title: Cloudflare Thanksgiving Intrusion
page: breaches/cloudflare-thanksgiving-2023.html
as-of: 2026-11-23
promise: Cloudflare left four Okta-leaked credentials valid, mistakenly believing them unused; with two, an intruder planted Sliver on its Atlassian server, and 76 repositories were archived there.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 4 | credentials left valid after the Okta compromise | One service token and three service accounts, out of thousands leaked
stat: ~8 | days in Jira and Confluence | November 15 to the alert on November 23 (this page's arithmetic)
stat: 76 | repositories archived to the Atlassian server | Of 120 viewed; not confirmed to have left, treated as exfiltrated
No customer data or systems impacted, per Cloudflare. Nation-state, in its belief ("likely a nation-state"); no country named.
notes:
Start with the figures the page leads with, and with whose figures they are.

Cloudflare says it failed to rotate four credentials out of the thousands leaked in the October 2023 Okta compromise: one Moveworks service token and three service accounts, for Smartsheet, Bitbucket and an AWS environment.

The intruder spent about eight days in Jira and Confluence, from 16:28 UTC on November 15 to the alert at 16:00 on November 23. That figure is the page's own arithmetic from Cloudflare's timeline.

It viewed 120 Bitbucket repositories and downloaded 76 of them to the Atlassian server. Cloudflare could not confirm whether those 76 left its network, and treats them as exfiltrated. Say both halves when you say the number.

Cloudflare says no customer data or systems were impacted, and that access was limited to the Atlassian suite and the server it runs on, though its own timeline also records access to a segmented AWS environment at step 02.

On attribution, keep Cloudflare's words. Based on its collaboration with colleagues in industry and government, it believes the attacker was a nation-state, "likely a nation-state" in the post's conclusion. It names no country, and ATT&CK does not cite the incident.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: October 18, 2023 | The Okta compromise reaches Cloudflare, which detects it
event: November 14, 09:22:49 | Probing: Cloudflare's Okta and Dashboard refuse; an AWS environment lets it in
event: November 15, 16:28:38 | Into Jira and Confluence with two leaked credentials
event: November 16, 14:36:37 | A look-alike Atlassian account created
event: November 22, 14:18:22 | Sliver installed through ScriptRunner; repositories archived over the next day
event: November 23, 16:00 | An automated alert on an admin-group change
Times are UTC. Code Red ran from November 27 to January 5. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
Every date on this slide is one the page gives, and the markers are evenly spaced, not to scale.

Cloudflare dates the start of the attack to October 18, 2023, when, for the second time, a compromise of Okta's systems reached Cloudflare and gave a threat actor a set of Cloudflare credentials. Cloudflare detected that intrusion the same day.

From 09:22:49 UTC on November 14 the actor probed for ways to use the leaked credentials. By the page's arithmetic that came 27 days after Cloudflare detected the Okta-related intrusion. At 16:28:38 on November 15 it authenticated through Cloudflare's gateway with the Moveworks service token and used the Smartsheet service account to get into Jira and Confluence. At 14:36:37 on November 16 it created an Atlassian account that looked like an ordinary Cloudflare user.

At 14:18:22 on November 22 it installed Sliver on the Atlassian server through the ScriptRunner for Jira plugin, and over the next day it archived 76 repositories to that server. At 16:00 on Thanksgiving Day, November 23, an automated alert told the security team that the Smartsheet account had been added to an administrator group.

Cloudflare published its account on February 1, 2024.

== Four credentials, and what each one reached
layout: table
row: Credential | What it reached, per Cloudflare | Where the page places it
row: Moveworks service token | Remote access into the Atlassian system | Step 03, through the gateway
row: Smartsheet service account | Administrative access to Jira | Steps 03 to 06 and 09
row: Bitbucket service account | The source code management system | Not said to be used, even at step 08
row: AWS environment service account | No global network access; no customer or sensitive data | Step 02, by this page's reading
Cloudflare: "mistakenly it was believed they were unused." It does not say how the four were obtained or why they were believed unused.
notes:
These are the four credentials Cloudflare failed to rotate, with what Cloudflare says each one reached.

The Moveworks service token granted remote access into Cloudflare's Atlassian system. The Smartsheet service account, used by that SaaS application, had administrative access to Cloudflare's Jira. The Bitbucket service account was used to reach the source code management system. The fourth was a service account for an AWS environment that, Cloudflare says, had no access to the global network and held no customer or sensitive data.

The post ties two of them to the Atlassian access: the Moveworks token got the actor through the gateway at step 03, and the Smartsheet account got it into Jira and Confluence and was used again at steps 04, 05, 06 and 09. The post does not say the Bitbucket account was used, even when repositories were archived at step 08. The page reads the AWS environment probed at step 02 as the fourth credential's, because Cloudflare's two descriptions match; Cloudflare does not say so in one sentence.

Cloudflare says they were skipped because it mistakenly believed they were unused, and calls the failure its own, not an error by Atlassian, AWS, Moveworks or Smartsheet. Neither post says how the four were obtained, and the page does not guess.

== Who says what, and how sure
layout: table
row: Claim | Whose it is | The caveat the page keeps
row: A nation-state attacker | Cloudflare, after work with industry and government | "Likely a nation-state"; no country named
row: 76 repositories exfiltrated | Cloudflare's working assumption | It could not confirm they left its network
row: Access limited to the Atlassian suite and its server | Cloudflare | Its own timeline also records the AWS environment at step 02
row: CrowdStrike found nothing missed | Cloudflare, reporting CrowdStrike | No public statement from CrowdStrike found; Reuters: no immediate reply
row: 194,100 wiki pages | Cloudflare's current post | The first version said 14,099 (Internet Archive)
notes:
This table keeps each claim's attribution next to it.

Cloudflare believes the attacker was a nation-state, based on its collaboration with colleagues in industry and government. The post's conclusion says "likely a nation-state." It names no country.

The 76 repositories are treated as exfiltrated because Cloudflare could not confirm whether they left its network and chose to assume the worst. They were downloaded to the Atlassian server; the page maps that as staging and maps no exfiltration technique.

Cloudflare says access was limited to the Atlassian suite and the server it runs on. Its own timeline also records the actor reaching the segmented AWS environment at step 02, which Cloudflare says held no customer or sensitive data. Cloudflare's separate claim, that no customer data or systems were impacted, is on the At a glance slide.

Cloudflare says CrowdStrike's independent assessment found no activity Cloudflare had missed and confirmed 10:44 on November 24 as the last evidence of the actor. The page found no public statement from CrowdStrike itself, and Reuters reported that CrowdStrike did not immediately respond to a request for comment.

The wiki total changed between versions of the post: the first gave 14,099 pages, the current text 194,100. The page follows the current text, and the Internet Archive holds both.

== The kill chain, stages 01 to 05
layout: flow
step: 01 | Credential Exposure | Four leaked credentials not rotated | T1199
step: 02 | Reconnaissance | Probing; one AWS environment reached | T1078.004
step: 03 | Initial Access | A service token through the gateway | T1550.001
step: 04 | Collection | 202 wiki pages and 36 tickets read | T1213.001
step: 05 | Persistence | A look-alike Atlassian account | T1136
Arrows show the page's stage order. The page puts the lesson in step 01.
notes:
The page has nine stages, so the chain takes two slides. Each step shows its phase and one ATT&CK technique the page maps to it.

Stage 01 is the exposure: credentials leaked in the Okta compromise were all meant to be rotated, and four were not. Stage 02 is the probing on November 14, when Cloudflare's Okta instance and Dashboard refused the actor and an AWS environment let it in. Stage 03 is initial access to Jira and Confluence, with the Moveworks service token through Cloudflare's gateway and then the Smartsheet service account. Stage 04 is the reading: 202 wiki pages and 36 Jira tickets. Stage 05 is persistence, an Atlassian account made to look like an ordinary Cloudflare user.

The page names step 01 as where the lesson sits, and on its reading that lesson is about how accurate a credential inventory is. We come back to it after the stages.

== The kill chain, stages 06 to 09
layout: flow
step: 06 | Execution | Sliver installed via ScriptRunner | T1059
step: 07 | Lateral Movement | Console server attempt, denied | T1021
step: 08 | Collection | 76 repositories archived | T1213.003
step: 09 | Detection | An admin-group change trips the alert | T1098
Cloudflare's timeline shows no alert before step 09.
notes:
The second half runs from the Atlassian server outwards, and then to the alert.

Stage 06 is execution: the Smartsheet account's Jira administrator rights and the ScriptRunner for Jira plugin put the Sliver command and control framework on the Atlassian server. Stage 07 is an attempt to move from there to a console server in a São Paulo data center that was not yet in production; it was denied. Stage 08 is collection from Bitbucket: 120 repositories viewed and 76 archived to the Atlassian server. Stage 09 is detection, an automated alert on the actor adding the Smartsheet account to an administrator group.

Cloudflare's timeline shows no earlier alert, whether on the account created at stage 05, the Sliver install at stage 06 or the archiving at stage 08. The post does not say whether rules for those events existed. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== Four leaked credentials the rotation skipped
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Credential Exposure
technique: T1199 | Trusted Relationship
fact: Exposure | the Okta compromise, detected at Cloudflare on October 18, 2023
fact: Not rotated | Moveworks token; Smartsheet, Bitbucket, AWS service accounts
fact: Reason given | "mistakenly it was believed they were unused"
fact: Not stated | how the four were obtained, or why they were believed unused
Thousands leaked, and all were meant to be rotated. An exemption from rotation rested on a belief about usage that was wrong.
notes:
The Okta support system kill chain on csoh.org covers the compromise behind this stage. Cloudflare's October post says that, it appears, the actor hijacked a session token from a support ticket a Cloudflare employee had created. Using it as an open Okta session with administrative privileges, the actor got into Cloudflare's own Okta instance and compromised two employee accounts there. Cloudflare detected that intrusion on October 18 and wrote at the time that it had contained it before the actor could establish persistence.

Cloudflare's February post says all the leaked credentials were meant to be rotated, and that it failed to rotate one service token and three service accounts out of thousands because it mistakenly believed they were unused. Those credentials were how the actor first got into its systems and kept access to its Atlassian products. Okta says it notified customers and shared guidance to rotate credentials on October 19, per TechRadar.

Neither post says how the four were obtained, whether from files in Okta's support system or during the October session, and the page does not guess.

ATT&CK has no technique for a rotation that left four entries valid. The page maps the supplier path, a compromise at Cloudflare's identity provider, to Trusted Relationship, and calls the failure underneath one of inventory accuracy.

== What a non-human identity is
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, NHI (Non-Human Identity) | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-nhi
A non-human identity is any identity that isn't a person: service accounts, OAuth tokens, API keys, workload identities and third-party SaaS integration credentials among them.

The discipline covers inventory, ownership, lifecycle, rotation, vaulting and behavior detection.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains a general idea from the csoh.org glossary.

The glossary defines a non-human identity as any identity that isn't a person. Its list includes service accounts, IAM roles, managed identities, OAuth tokens, API keys, certificates, workload identities, third-party SaaS integration credentials, bots and AI agents.

It says the security discipline around them covers inventory, ownership, lifecycle, rotation, vaulting and behavior detection.

The page tags stages 01 and 03 Non-Human Identity. All four credentials in this chain were non-human: one service token and three service accounts. Two belonged to integrations, Moveworks and the SaaS application Smartsheet, rather than to people.

Read this slide as the general idea only. Inventory and rotation are the two parts of that discipline the page's lesson turns on.

== Probing for a way to use them, November 14
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Reconnaissance
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
fact: Refused | Cloudflare's Okta instance; the Cloudflare Dashboard
fact: Reached | AWS environment behind Cloudflare Apps, segmented (Cloudflare)
fact: Fourth credential | fits Cloudflare's account of the fourth (this page's reading)
fact: Primary IOC | 193.142.58[.]126, M247, Bucharest; not tied to a step
Cloudflare's timeline lists no alert on these attempts. The first alert it describes came nine days later.
notes:
Cloudflare's logs show the actor probing its systems from November 14, looking for ways to use the credentials and for systems they could reach. It tried Cloudflare's Okta instance and was denied, and tried the Cloudflare Dashboard and was denied.

One attempt worked: the actor accessed an AWS environment used to power the Cloudflare Apps marketplace. Cloudflare says that environment was segmented, with no access to the global network or customer data, that the service account for it was revoked, and that it validated the environment's integrity. It does not say what the actor did there or when the account was revoked.

Cloudflare does not say in one sentence that this account was the fourth unrotated credential. Its list of the four ends with an AWS environment with no access to the global network and no customer or sensitive data, and it describes this one the same way, so the page reads them as the same environment. Keep that label.

Cloudflare's published indicators name 193.142.58[.]126, at M247 Europe SRL in Bucharest, as the actor's primary infrastructure, without tying it to particular steps.

The page suggests treating any authentication with a credential on the exposed list as an incident, whether it succeeds or fails, and alerting on the first use of a dormant non-human credential.

== A service token through the gateway
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1550.001 | Use Alternate Authentication Material: Application Access Token
technique: T1078 | Valid Accounts
fact: First credential | Moveworks service token, through Cloudflare's gateway
fact: Second credential | Smartsheet service account, with administrative access to Jira
fact: Reached | Jira and Confluence on the self-hosted Atlassian server
fact: Hardware keys | not involved: software presents a token (this page's reading)
Both integration credentials still worked on November 15, almost a month after the Okta compromise.
notes:
Cloudflare says the actor reached Jira and Confluence on November 15 by authenticating through its gateway with the Moveworks service token, then used the Smartsheet service account to get into the Atlassian suite. Both belonged to integrations rather than to people.

Cloudflare credits its access controls, firewall rules and hardware security keys, enforced through its own Zero Trust tools, for the actor's failure to reach many other systems. The page's reading, which Cloudflare does not discuss: hardware keys protect people, and a service token is presented by software, so no hardware key is involved when it is used. What limits a token is where it is accepted from, what it can reach and how long it stays valid. Cloudflare does not say what restrictions applied to this one. Whatever they were, it was accepted from the actor's connection, it reached the Atlassian system, and it was still valid.

The Smartsheet account lived on Cloudflare's self-hosted Atlassian server, so the page maps the parent Valid Accounts technique.

The page suggests alerting when an integration's credential is used from outside that integration's own network, or used to browse, and, where the gateway allows it, accepting each service token only from its integration's source addresses.

== Reading the wiki and Jira for ways in
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Collection
technique: T1213.001 | Data from Information Repositories: Confluence
technique: T1213 | Data from Information Repositories
fact: Searched for | remote access, secret, token, openconnect, cloudflared
fact: Read | 202 of 194,100 wiki pages; 36 of 2,059,357 Jira tickets
fact: Tickets on | secret rotation, MFA bypass, the Okta response, and more
fact: Not targeted | customer data or customer configurations (Cloudflare)
Cloudflare reads this as an actor studying its global network to get a deeper foothold.
notes:
Once inside, the actor searched Cloudflare's wiki for terms including remote access, secret, client-secret, openconnect, cloudflared and token. It read 202 of the wiki's 194,100 pages and 36 of the 2,059,357 tickets in Jira, Cloudflare's bug database. The tickets covered vulnerability management, secret rotation, MFA bypass, network access and Cloudflare's response to the Okta incident itself.

On the day after first access the actor turned to the configuration and management of Cloudflare's global network. Cloudflare says its searches and the pages it read suggest interest in every aspect of access to Cloudflare's systems: password resets, remote access, configuration and Cloudflare's use of Salt. Cloudflare says it did not target customer data or customer configurations.

The searches look for secrets, but Cloudflare does not say any credential was found in what the actor read, so the page does not map Unsecured Credentials. During Code Red, Cloudflare searched Jira tickets and source code for secrets, and examined and deleted every HAR file uploaded to its wiki in case it held tokens.

Jira has no sub-technique of its own in ATT&CK, so its tickets map to the parent technique. The page suggests alerting on a service account searching a wiki at all, and on searches for secret-like terms by any account.

== An account that looked like a colleague
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Persistence
technique: T1136 | Create Account
technique: T1036.010 | Masquerading: Masquerade Account Name
technique: T1098 | Account Manipulation
fact: When | November 16, 14:36:37 UTC
fact: Account | looks like a normal Cloudflare user; in several groups
fact: Purpose | survive removal of the Smartsheet account (Cloudflare)
fact: Quiet period | November 17 to 20, apart from apparently brief access tests
Created by the Smartsheet account as a second way in, in case the first was found. On November 23 both were.
notes:
Cloudflare says the new account was made to look like an ordinary Cloudflare user and was added to a number of Atlassian groups, so that the actor would keep access to the Atlassian environment if the Smartsheet service account were removed. It was a second way in, prepared in case the first was found.

On November 23 both were found. The Smartsheet account was deactivated at 16:35 and the actor's account at 17:23, 48 minutes later.

From 14:33:52 UTC on November 17 to 09:26:53 on November 20 the actor stayed away, apart from what Cloudflare says were apparently brief tests that its access still worked, and came back just before Thanksgiving. The post's summary places that further access on November 20 and 21.

Atlassian ran on Cloudflare's own server, so the page maps the parent Create Account technique rather than its Cloud Account sub-technique.

The page suggests alerting on an Atlassian account created anywhere but the identity provider's provisioning, and on any account created by a service account.

== Sliver installed through ScriptRunner
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Execution
technique: T1059 | Command and Scripting Interpreter
technique: T1105 | Ingress Tool Transfer
fact: When | November 22, 14:18:22 UTC
fact: Rights used | the Smartsheet service account's administrative access to Jira
fact: Payload | jvm-agent, served from idowall[.]com
fact: C2 server | 198.244.174[.]214, OVH SAS, London
Jira administrator plus a scripting plugin meant code execution on the server.
notes:
Cloudflare connects the two facts directly. Because the Smartsheet service account had administrative access to Jira, the actor could install the Sliver Adversary Emulation Framework, and it did so with the ScriptRunner for Jira plugin. Sliver gave the actor continuous access to the Atlassian server itself.

Cloudflare's indicators name the payload, a file called jvm-agent with SHA256 bdd1a085d651082ad567b03e5186d1d46d822bb7794157ab8cce95d850a3caaf, the domain that served it, idowall[.]com, and the Sliver C2 server, 198.244.174[.]214 at OVH SAS in London.

The page calls this the step where an application permission became control of a host. An integration account that was a Jira administrator, on a server where a scripting plugin was installed, could run code on the machine that hosted Jira.

Cloudflare does not say whether ScriptRunner was already installed, what script the actor ran, which protocol Sliver used for C2, or how it would have survived a restart. So the page maps the parent Command and Scripting Interpreter technique and maps no C2 protocol.

The page suggests logging and alerting on every ScriptRunner script run, and alerting on new outbound destinations from the Atlassian server.

== What Sliver is
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK, Sliver (S0633) | https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0633/
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entry C2
MITRE ATT&CK: Sliver is an open source, cross-platform, red team command and control (C2) framework written in Golang.

ATT&CK records C2 over HTTP and HTTPS, DNS and the WireGuard VPN protocol.
notes:
This is a background slide, from MITRE ATT&CK's software entry S0633, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes Sliver as an open source, cross-platform, red team command and control framework written in Golang, and lists Windows, Linux and macOS. Its technique entries record that Sliver can support C2 over HTTP and HTTPS, over DNS, and over the WireGuard VPN protocol, and that it can download additional content and files from the Sliver server to the client on the victim machine.

The csoh.org glossary defines C2, command and control, as the channel an attacker uses to control implants, often blended with legitimate cloud services.

The page says Sliver is a framework red teams and attackers both use for command and control. It does not say which protocol Sliver used here, so the page maps no C2 protocol, and this slide's list is what the tool can do in general.

Also from the csoh.org glossary entry C2: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-c2

== A console server attempt, denied
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1021 | Remote Services
fact: Target | non-production console server, new São Paulo data center
fact: Path | Cloudflare ties the attempt to a non-enforced ACL
fact: Also failed | metrics, network configuration, build, alerting, release management
fact: Not stated | what denied it, or which protocol was tried
Manufacturers found nothing in the Brazil hardware; Cloudflare replaced it anyway.
notes:
Cloudflare says the actor used its access to the Atlassian server to attempt lateral movement, including an attempt on a non-production console server in its São Paulo, Brazil data center, which the post attributes to an ACL that was not enforced. The data center was new and not yet in production. The access was denied, and the actor reached none of the global network.

Cloudflare's logs also show attempts on its internal metrics, network configuration, build system, alerting systems and release management system, none of them successful by its review. Cloudflare credits access controls, firewall rules and hardware security keys enforced through its Zero Trust tools for those failures. That is a general statement: the post does not say what denied the console server attempt or what protocol was tried, so the page maps the parent Remote Services technique as an attempt.

Cloudflare still sent the Brazil equipment back to its manufacturers, whose forensic teams examined it for access or persistence. They found nothing, and Cloudflare replaced the hardware anyway.

The page suggests alerting on connection attempts from an application server to out-of-band management, and treating an ACL that exists but is not enforced as a finding before a site goes live.

== 76 repositories archived from Bitbucket
layout: stage
stage: 08
phase: Collection
technique: T1213.003 | Data from Information Repositories: Code Repositories
technique: T1074.001 | Data Staged: Local Data Staging
fact: Archived | 76 of 120 viewed, onto the Atlassian server
fact: Exfiltration | not confirmed; Cloudflare treats the 76 as exfiltrated
fact: Which path | not stated; the leaked Bitbucket account is not said to be used
Cloudflare's focus was embedded secrets and vulnerabilities, rather than someone having the code.
notes:
Over the day after Sliver went in, the actor viewed 120 of Cloudflare's 11,904 code repositories in Bitbucket and used the git archive feature on 76 to download them to the Atlassian server. Cloudflare could not confirm whether they were exfiltrated, and decided to treat them as exfiltrated.

It says the 76 were almost all about how backups work, how the global network is configured and managed, how identity works at Cloudflare, remote access, and its use of Terraform and Kubernetes. A small number held encrypted secrets, which were rotated immediately although they were strongly encrypted.

The leaked Bitbucket service account is one of the four, but the post does not say it was used here. In the timeline, Bitbucket access comes only after Sliver. Because no data was confirmed to have left, the page maps the archiving as staging and maps no exfiltration technique.

As a SaaS company, Cloudflare says, it considers its source code less precious than that of companies that distribute software to end users. During Code Red it reviewed all 76 repositories for embedded secrets and vulnerabilities and rotated the secrets it found in code.

The page suggests alerting on one identity archiving dozens of repositories in a day, and on archives written to a server that is not a build host.

== One admin-group change trips the alert
layout: stage
stage: 09
phase: Detection
technique: T1098 | Account Manipulation
fact: Alert fired on | Smartsheet account added to an admin group, 15:58 UTC
fact: Earlier alerts | none in the timeline: not the account, Sliver or the archiving
fact: Rules for those | the post does not say whether any existed
fact: After deactivation | last known activity 10:44, November 24; Sliver removed 11:59
On this page's reading, disabling the accounts did not by itself end access while the implant was still on the server.
notes:
At 15:58 UTC on November 23 the actor added the Smartsheet service account to an administrator group, and an automated alert reached the security team two minutes later. The alert that worked fired on a privilege change.

Cloudflare's timeline shows no earlier alert, whether on the account created on November 16, the Sliver install on November 22 or the archiving of 76 repositories, and the post does not say whether rules for those events existed.

Both accounts were deactivated that afternoon and the known addresses were blocked that evening. The last known activity, at 10:44 on November 24, came after all of that, and Sliver was removed at 11:59. The page reads the timeline as showing that disabling the accounts did not by itself end access while the implant was still on the server. Keep that labelled as the page's reading.

Cloudflare's own team began investigating on November 23 and brought in CrowdStrike's forensic team on November 26. Cloudflare says CrowdStrike's assessment confirmed 10:44 on November 24 as the last evidence of the actor. The page found no public statement from CrowdStrike itself.

The next slide sets out the hours in order.

== Thanksgiving Day to the last activity, in UTC
layout: timeline
event: November 23, 15:58 | The actor adds the Smartsheet account to an administrator group
event: 16:00 | Automated alert; the SOC starts investigating at 16:12
event: 16:35 and 17:23 | Smartsheet account, then the look-alike account, deactivated
event: 21:31 | Firewall rules block the actor's known IP addresses
event: November 24, 10:44 | Last known actor activity, confirmed by CrowdStrike per Cloudflare
event: November 24, 11:59 | Sliver removed
Cloudflare declared an internal incident at 17:43. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
Every time on this slide is one the page gives, in UTC. The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

At 15:58 on November 23 the actor added the Smartsheet service account to an administrator group. The automated alert reached the security team at 16:00, and Cloudflare's SOC started investigating at 16:12.

The SOC deactivated the Smartsheet account at 16:35, 35 minutes after the alert, and found and deactivated the actor's look-alike Atlassian account at 17:23, 48 minutes after that. Cloudflare declared an internal incident at 17:43. Firewall rules blocked the actor's known IP addresses at 21:31.

The last known actor activity was at 10:44 on November 24, and Sliver was removed at 11:59 that day. Cloudflare says CrowdStrike confirmed 10:44 as the last evidence of the actor.

Point at the gap between 17:23 and 10:44. On the page's reading, deactivating both accounts did not by itself end access while Sliver was still on the server.

== The response went far wider than four credentials
layout: stats
stat: 5,000+ | production credentials rotated | Every one of them
stat: 4,893 | systems given forensic triage | Every global network machine reimaged
stat: 76 | repositories reviewed | For embedded secrets and vulnerabilities
Code Red ran November 27 to January 5. Cloudflare says CrowdStrike's assessment found no activity Cloudflare had missed.
notes:
Detection came from the attacker's own privilege change, and the response went far wider than four credentials.

From November 27 to January 5 Cloudflare ran a company-wide effort it called Code Red. It rotated every production credential, more than 5,000 of them, physically segmented its test and staging systems, carried out forensic triage on 4,893 systems, and reimaged and rebooted every machine in its global network and all its Atlassian products.

It reviewed all 76 repositories for embedded secrets and vulnerabilities and rotated the secrets it found in code. It also looked for software packages that had not been updated, user accounts that might have been created, and unused active employee accounts.

Cloudflare says CrowdStrike's independent assessment found no activity Cloudflare had missed. The page found no public statement from CrowdStrike itself, so keep that attributed to Cloudflare.

== The lesson sits in step 01: how accurate the inventory is
layout: text
On this page's reading, the lesson is the accuracy of the credential inventory. A belief that four credentials were unused decided which stayed valid, and one of them held administrator rights in Jira.
notes:
The page puts the lesson in step 01, and labels it as its own reading.

Cloudflare's own words are about a belief. All the leaked credentials were meant to be rotated, and these four, out of thousands, were not, because it was mistakenly believed they were unused. That one belief decided which credentials stayed valid.

A credential marked unused still works for whoever holds it until it is revoked. By the page's arithmetic, the actor began probing for ways to use these 27 days after Cloudflare detected the Okta-related intrusion.

The page draws three conclusions. Rotation after a breach has to cover every exposed credential. A credential exempted because it looks unused has to be shown to be unused from its last-used data, and then revoked rather than left valid. And an accurate entry also records what the credential reaches: one of the four "unused" credentials held administrator rights in Jira.

Cloudflare does not say why the four were believed unused; it says only that the belief was wrong. Do not fill that gap when you present this.

== Six defenses against stages 01 to 05
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05
row: Rotate every exposed credential; prove "unused" | 01
row: Revoke what is unused | 01
row: Record what each credential reaches | 01 | 05
row: Alert on what came before the alert | 05
row: Watch the exposed list after rotating | 02
row: Assume the worst about what was read | 04
None of the page's eight defenses names stage 03. Three of these also name stages 06 to 09.
notes:
The page gives eight defenses. Six of them reach the first five stages, and a cell is marked only where the defense's own words name something from that stage.

Rotating every exposed credential and revoking what is unused both name the four credentials left valid at stage 01. Recording what each credential reaches names the Smartsheet credential, one of the four, and the look-alike user it created at stage 05. Alerting on what came before names the account created on November 16, stage 05. Watching the exposed list names the refused sign-ins nine days before the alert, stage 02. Assuming the worst about what was read names the actor's wiki searches and Cloudflare's search of Jira tickets, stage 04.

Now look at the empty column. None of the page's eight defenses names stage 03, where the Moveworks service token was accepted through the gateway. Two near misses are not marked. The rotation defense says the gateway's authentication logs show when a service token was last used, and the watchlist defense flags any use of a credential the inventory says nothing uses; neither names a stage 03 detail. That gap is the second discussion question.

== Five defenses against stages 06 to 09
layout: matrix
columns: 06 | 07 | 08 | 09
row: Record what each credential reaches | 06 | 09
row: Treat a scripting plugin as code execution | 06
row: Alert on what came before the alert | 06 | 08 | 09
row: Enforce segmentation before go-live | 07
row: Assume the worst about what was read | 08
Every stage from 06 to 09 has a defense; three meet stage 06.
notes:
These five defenses cover the second half of the chain.

Recording what each credential reaches names the Smartsheet account installing Sliver at stage 06 and being added to an administrator group at stage 09. Treating a scripting plugin as code execution names ScriptRunner and Sliver on the Atlassian server, stage 06. Alerting on what came before names the Sliver install and its outbound destination at stage 06, the archiving of 76 repositories at stage 08, and the administrator-group change that did fire, at stage 09. Enforcing segmentation names the São Paulo console server and the ACL that was not enforced, stage 07. Assuming the worst names the 76 repositories treated as exfiltrated, stage 08.

Three defenses meet stage 06, where an integration account's Jira administrator rights became code execution on the host.

The next four slides take the defenses in pairs.

== Rotate everything exposed, and revoke what is unused
layout: compare
col: Rotate the whole exposed set | The exposed list is the scope: rotate or revoke every entry | Exempt none on someone's understanding of what it does | Check last use first: IAM credential report, aws iam get-access-key-last-used | Gateway tokens and SaaS accounts: their authentication logs
col: Revoke what is unused | If it is unused, revoking it breaks nothing | If revoking breaks something, the inventory was wrong (this page's reasoning) | Avoid a valid credential nobody is watching | NHI Mgmt Group and OWASP NHI1: retire or decommission it
notes:
Two defenses for stage 01.

Rotate every credential in the exposed set, and prove "unused" before acting on it. After a breach notice the exposed list is the scope: every entry is rotated or revoked, and none is exempted on someone's understanding of what it does. Where a credential looks unused, check the record of its last use first. In AWS, the IAM credential report and aws iam get-access-key-last-used give last-used dates for access keys; for gateway service tokens and SaaS integration accounts, the gateway's and the application's authentication logs answer the same question. NHI Mgmt Group's case page makes both points, and OWASP's Non-Human Identities Top 10, under Vulnerable Third-Party NHI, says to keep a playbook to revoke and reissue every connected OAuth token.

Revoke what is unused instead of leaving it valid. If a credential really is unused, revoking it breaks nothing; if revoking it breaks something, the inventory was wrong, and the breakage says so at once. That is the page's reasoning. These four sat valid and unwatched for about a month. NHI Mgmt Group says to retire it, and OWASP's Improper Offboarding entry says to recertify non-human identities periodically and decommission any no longer needed.

== Record what a credential reaches; treat scripting plugins as code execution
layout: compare
col: Record reach; limit admin | An "unused" Smartsheet credential held Jira administrator | Record what each non-human identity can reach | Narrowest permissions; review admin groups on a schedule
col: Scripting is code execution | Adaptavist: by default, running a script takes Jira administrator | The Script Console can run programs on the server | Adaptavist: a setting limits script editing to System admins | This page: turn it on; alert on script runs; limit outbound routes
notes:
Two defenses for the Smartsheet account's rights and what they unlocked.

Record what each credential reaches, and keep integration accounts out of administrator roles. One of the four "unused" credentials belonged to Smartsheet's integration and held administrative access to Jira. The actor used that one account to create a look-alike user and install Sliver, and at the end added it to an administrator group. An inventory entry recording its reach marks it high-impact whatever its usage. Give integration accounts the narrowest permissions their integration needs, and review administrator-group membership for non-human accounts on a schedule.

Treat a scripting plugin as code execution on the host. Adaptavist, ScriptRunner's vendor, says in its security documentation that by default authoring or running a script takes Jira administrator permissions, and that the Script Console can run a command-line program on the Jira server. Its "Enable System Admin Only Script Edit Permissions" setting limits script editing to groups that hold Jira System administrator permissions.

The page then suggests listing the plugins that run scripts on each application server, turning on restrictions like that one, keeping integration accounts out of the groups they allow, alerting on every script run, and giving the server only the outbound routes it needs, so an implant calling home fails or stands out.

== Alert on what came before; watch the exposed list
layout: compare
col: Alert on what came before | An account created outside the identity provider's provisioning (05) | A new outbound destination from the Atlassian server (06) | One identity archiving dozens of repositories in a day (08)
col: Watch the exposed list | Refused sign-ins nine days before the alert (02) | High confidence: a leaked credential refused, or an "unused" one used | Keep the set on a watchlist for months
Both are this page's suggestions.
notes:
Two detection defenses, both the page's suggestions.

Alert on what came before the alert that fired. Cloudflare's detection fired on the administrator-group change at 15:58 on November 23. Its timeline shows no earlier alert on the account created on November 16, the Sliver install on November 22 or the archiving of 76 repositories, and the post does not say whether rules for them existed. Each has a clean signal: an Atlassian account created outside the identity provider's provisioning, a new outbound destination from the Atlassian server, where the Sliver C2 server sat at OVH in London, and one identity archiving dozens of repositories in a day. Route them to the SIEM, the security information and event management system, with the same priority as the privilege change.

Watch the exposed list after rotating it. Nine days before the alert, the actor, probing for ways to use the leaked credentials, tried to sign in to Cloudflare's Okta instance and the Cloudflare Dashboard and was refused. A refused sign-in with a credential known to have leaked is a high-confidence signal, and so is any use of a credential the inventory says nothing uses. Put the exposed set on a watchlist and keep it there for months after the breach.

== Segment before go-live; keep secrets out of what can be read
layout: compare
col: Enforce segmentation before go-live | São Paulo: an ACL not enforced, though the attempt was denied | This page: keep out-of-band management unreachable from application servers | This page: treat an unenforced ACL as a pre-launch finding
col: Assume the worst about what was read | Cloudflare treated the 76 as exfiltrated and reviewed them | It rotated the secrets it found, encrypted ones included | It searched tickets and code for secrets, and deleted wiki HAR files | Start your own review where the actor searched: remote access, secrets, tokens
notes:
Two defenses for what the actor tried to reach and what it read.

Enforce segmentation before a site goes live. Cloudflare ties the attempt on the São Paulo console server to an ACL that was not enforced. The attempt was still denied, and the post does not say what denied it. The page suggests keeping out-of-band management unreachable from application servers, and treating an ACL that exists but is not enforced as a pre-launch finding. Cloudflare credits access controls, firewall rules and hardware security keys enforced through its Zero Trust tools for the actor's failures elsewhere in its network.

Assume the worst about what was read, and keep secrets out of what can be read. Cloudflare treated the 76 repositories as exfiltrated, reviewed them for embedded secrets and vulnerabilities, and rotated the secrets it found, encrypted ones included. It searched Jira tickets and source code for secrets and deleted every HAR file uploaded to its wiki.

The actor's wiki searches were for remote access, secrets and tokens, and the page says that is where a review of your own wiki and ticket system should start.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: After a breach notice, is every exposed credential rotated or revoked, with none exempted?
check: Can you prove a credential is unused from its last-used data?
check: Do you revoke unused credentials, or leave them valid?
check: Does your inventory record what each non-human identity can reach?
check: Do any integration accounts sit in administrator groups?
check: Which plugins can run scripts on your application servers, and who can use them?
check: Would an account created outside provisioning, or a leaked credential's use, alert anyone?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether, after the last breach notice that touched you, every exposed credential was rotated or revoked, and whether any was exempted because someone believed nothing used it. Ask whether you could prove a credential unused from last-used data, such as the IAM credential report or a gateway's authentication logs, and whether unused credentials are revoked or left valid.

Ask whether your inventory records what each non-human identity can reach, and whether any integration account sits in an administrator group.

Ask which plugins can run scripts on your application servers, who is allowed to use them, whether each script run is logged, and whether those servers can reach any destination they do not need.

Finally, ask whether an account created outside your identity provider's provisioning would alert anyone, and whether any use of a credential on an exposed list, refused or not, would.

== Which of your unused credentials would still work today?
layout: question
Cloudflare left four leaked credentials valid, mistakenly believing them unused; one held administrator rights in Jira. Pick an integration credential in your estate. How do you know whether anything uses it, and what it reaches?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one.

The page's facts: all the credentials leaked in the Okta compromise were meant to be rotated, and four, out of thousands, were not, because Cloudflare mistakenly believed they were unused. Cloudflare does not say why. One of them, the Smartsheet integration's service account, held administrative access to Jira, and the actor used it to create a look-alike account, install Sliver and finally join an administrator group. By the page's arithmetic, the actor began probing for ways to use the four 27 days after Cloudflare detected the Okta-related intrusion.

Ask each person to pick one integration credential, a service token or a SaaS integration's service account, and say two things before you show the answer slide: what record would tell them it is unused, and what it can reach if someone else holds it.

== Prove it unused, revoke it, and record its reach
layout: answer
From the page's defenses:
- Rotate or revoke every exposed credential, and exempt none.
- Check last-used data before calling anything unused.
- Revoke what is unused, and record what each credential reaches.
notes:
The page's answer has three parts.

First, the scope. After a breach notice the exposed list is the scope, and every entry is rotated or revoked. None is exempted on someone's understanding of what it does.

Second, the evidence. A credential that looks unused has to be shown to be unused from its last-used data: the IAM credential report and aws iam get-access-key-last-used in AWS, and the gateway's and the application's authentication logs for service tokens and integration accounts. Then revoke it rather than leave it valid. If revoking it breaks something, the inventory was wrong, and the breakage says so at once; that is the page's reasoning.

Third, the reach. An entry that records what a non-human identity can reach would have marked the Smartsheet account as high-impact whatever its usage, because it held administrator rights in Jira.

Bring the group's answers back to these. If someone's only evidence that a credential is unused is that nobody remembers using it, that is the belief this chain turned on.

== What would you add for stage 03?
layout: question
None of the page's eight defenses names stage 03, where the Moveworks service token was accepted through Cloudflare's gateway almost a month after the Okta compromise. What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the empty column in the first defense matrix.

At stage 03, on November 15, the actor authenticated through Cloudflare's gateway with the Moveworks service token and then used the Smartsheet service account to get into Jira and Confluence. Both credentials belonged to integrations, and both still worked almost a month after the Okta compromise.

Cloudflare credits hardware security keys, among other controls, for the actor's failures elsewhere. The page reads that hardware keys protect people, while a service token is presented by software, so no hardware key is involved when it is used. Cloudflare does not say what restrictions applied to this token.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught: the gateway, the token's source, its reach or its age.

== The page's defenses do not name stage 03
layout: answer
This deck adds none. The stage's detect line is the page's only stage 03 suggestion:
- Alert on a token used from outside its integration's network, or used to browse.
- Where the gateway allows, accept it only from its integration's addresses.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. None of the page's eight defenses names stage 03, and this deck adds none of its own.

The stage itself carries the page's reading: what limits a token is where it is accepted from, what it can reach and how long it stays valid. Cloudflare does not discuss this. Whatever restrictions applied, the token was accepted from the actor's connection, it reached the Atlassian system, and it was still valid.

The stage's detect line is the page's suggestion: alert when an integration's credential is used from outside that integration's own network, or used to browse, and, where the gateway allows it, accept each service token only from its integration's source addresses.

The group may argue that rotating every exposed credential at stage 01 would have made the token useless. The page ties that defense to stage 01, not to the gateway, so keep that argument the group's own.

== Primary sources
layout: links
link: Cloudflare, Thanksgiving 2023 security incident | https://blog.cloudflare.com/thanksgiving-2023-security-incident/
link: Internet Archive, the first capture of that post | https://web.archive.org/web/20240201200520/https://blog.cloudflare.com/thanksgiving-2023-security-incident/
link: Cloudflare, how it mitigated another Okta compromise | https://blog.cloudflare.com/how-cloudflare-mitigated-yet-another-okta-compromise/
link: Okta Security, support system root cause and remediation | https://sec.okta.com/articles/2023/11/unauthorized-access-oktas-support-case-management-system-root-cause/
link: TechRadar Pro, with Okta's statement | https://www.techradar.com/pro/security/cloudflare-hacked-company-reveals-details-of-november-2023-cyberattack-blames-previous-okta-breach
link: Reuters, via Insurance Journal | https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2024/02/02/759164.htm
link: NHI Mgmt Group, Cloudflare case page | https://nhimg.org/cloudflare-breach
link: Adaptavist, ScriptRunner for Jira security | https://docs.adaptavist.com/sr4js/latest/get-started/vulnerabilities-and-security/
notes:
These are primary sources the csoh.org kill chain cites. Every fact about the incident in this deck comes from that page, which draws on these.

Cloudflare's February 1, 2024 post carries the timeline, the four credentials, the attribution and Code Red. The Internet Archive's first capture of it gives the wiki total as 14,099 pages, where the current text says 194,100. Cloudflare's October post covers the Okta compromise at Cloudflare, and Okta's root cause post covers its own support system. TechRadar carries Okta's statement, and Reuters reported that CrowdStrike did not immediately respond. NHI Mgmt Group and Adaptavist supply defense advice.

The page also cites two entries from OWASP's Non-Human Identities Top 10, Improper Offboarding and Vulnerable Third-Party NHI, and MITRE ATT&CK pages for three techniques and for Sliver. They are left off this slide for space.

The background slides name their own sources: the csoh.org glossary and MITRE ATT&CK.

== Related kill chain and defense topics
layout: links
link: Okta support system (2023) | breaches/okta-support-har.html
link: Non-human identity | non-human-identity.html
link: IAM & cloud identity | iam.html
link: SaaS security (SSPM) | saas-security.html
link: Zero trust | zero-trust.html
link: Detection engineering | detection-engineering.html
link: Incident response | incident-response.html
Step 01 follows from the Okta support system kill chain, the compromise that exposed the four credentials.
notes:
The page links one other kill chain and six defense topics.

The Okta support system kill chain covers the compromise this chain starts from. An attacker inside Okta's customer support system took session tokens from files that customers had uploaded, and Cloudflare's October post says that, it appears, one came from a support ticket a Cloudflare employee had created. Both kill chains give the same date for the October part: Cloudflare detected the intrusion into its own Okta instance on October 18. Neither post says how the four credentials in this chain were obtained.

The defense topics cover non-human identity, identity and access management in the cloud, SaaS security and SaaS security posture management, zero trust, detection engineering, and incident response.
